+**APPLIED (2026-06-17, branch `fix/rbs-lm-bigram-resonator-s57`).** Done in BOTH the origin `_rbs_lm_inference.py` (ours-first) and the package `srmech/rbs_lm/inference.py` (the §9 mirror), one commit: deleted the `Counter`/`defaultdict` import, the `bigram_counts`/`next_after` fields, the count loop in `learn`, and the `next_after`-gate in `next_token_distribution`. The candidate set is now `self.vocab` (the bounded atom codebook); recovery is `probe = klein4_unbind(M, encode_context(context[-k:]))` → `sim_k4_batch(probe, vocab_vecs)` → softmax, with §56 `T<=0` → argmax. Everything else (M, vocab_vecs, encode_context, sim_k4_batch, temperature, F154 capacity subsample) untouched. **Acceptance (the honest-risk gate): grounded next-token recovery from M ALONE, no n-gram gate, per-tome = 100%** — greedy recall 27/27, 57/57, 57/57, 117/117 over vocab∈{30,60,60,120} at D∈{16384,16384,32768,32768}; the autoregressive `infer(T=0)` reproduces the grounded continuation (`t0,t1,t2 → t3..t7`). Recall did NOT degrade at per-tome scale — the resonator over the bounded atom set recovers the right factor unaided (confirming the per-tome F778/F834 architecture is what bounds the candidate set structurally). The numpy in this module is OUT of §57's scope (the spec's "numpy-free already" line does not match the actual file — the `_softmax`/`vocab_vecs`/`M` still use `np.*`; that's a separate concern, left untouched here).
